::STAT_U01_L06_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A relative frequency of 0.25 is equal to what percent?
{
=A. 25%#Multiply 0.25 by 100 to convert to percent.
~B. 2.5%#This moves the decimal one place too few.
~C. 250%#This moves the decimal too far.
~D. 75%#75% is the complement of 25%, not the conversion.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
In a segmented bar graph, each complete bar usually represents what?
{
~A. One category only#A complete bar contains all categories for a group.
~B. The largest raw count#The bar length is standardized, usually to 100%.
=C. 100% of one group#Each bar is split into category segments that total the whole group.
~D. The number of survey questions#The bar represents a group total, not question count.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A table shows 60% yes and 40% no. Which segment should be longer?
{
~A. No#No is only 40%.
=B. Yes#60% is larger than 40%, so the yes segment is longer.
~C. Both equal#The percentages are not equal.
~D. Neither, because percentages cannot be graphed#Percentages can be shown in segmented bars.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Why should the same category order be used in each segmented bar?
{
~A. To change counts into decimals#Order does not perform conversion.
~B. To make all segments equal#Segment sizes still depend on relative frequencies.
~C. To hide the smallest category#Displays should communicate clearly, not hide categories.
=D. To make comparisons across groups easier#Consistent order helps viewers compare matching categories.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A group has 12 yes responses out of 30 total. What is the relative frequency for yes?
{
~A. 12%#The count is 12, but the percent uses 12 divided by 30.
~B. 30%#The total is 30, not the percent yes.
=C. 40%#12 divided by 30 equals 0.40, or 40%.
~D. 60%#60% would represent 18 out of 30.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
When are relative frequencies better than raw counts?
{
=A. When group sizes are different#Relative frequencies compare proportions fairly across unequal totals.
~B. When all data are numerical measurements#Relative frequency tables are often used for categorical data.
~C. When no categories exist#Categories are needed for these comparisons.
~D. When the display has no labels#Labels are still required.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Grade 9 has 30% walk and Grade 10 has 15% walk. What comparison is supported?
{
~A. Grade 9 has exactly twice as many walkers#Percentages compare proportions, not raw counts.
~B. Grade 10 has more walkers#15% is a smaller proportion than 30%.
~C. Both grades have equal walking proportions#The percentages are different.
=D. Grade 9 has a greater proportion of walkers#30% is greater than 15%.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
What should the segments in one bar add to when using percentages?
{
~A. 50%#A complete group should total 100%, not 50%.
~B. The largest category only#The whole bar includes all categories.
=C. 100%#The segments represent all categories within that group.
~D. The number of categories#Percent totals are based on the whole, not category count.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A segmented bar has 20%, 30%, and 50% segments. Which is the largest segment?
{
~A. 20%#20% is the smallest of the listed values.
=B. 50%#50% is the greatest relative frequency.
~C. 30%#30% is less than 50%.
~D. All are equal#The percentages are different.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
What does a segmented bar graph display from a relative frequency table?
{
~A. Only individual data values#The graph summarizes groups.
=B. How category proportions make up each group#Segment lengths represent category shares within each group.
~C. Only medians and quartiles#That is a box plot purpose.
~D. Only paired numerical points#That is a scatterplot purpose.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A relative frequency table lists 0.10, 0.30, and 0.60. What should these become on a percent bar?
{
=A. 10%, 30%, and 60%#Each decimal is multiplied by 100.
~B. 1%, 3%, and 6%#This conversion is too small by a factor of 10.
~C. 100%, 300%, and 600%#This conversion is too large.
~D. 90%, 70%, and 40%#These are complements, not conversions.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A segmented bar shows Group A has a larger music segment than Group B. What does that mean?
{
=A. A greater proportion of Group A chose music#Segment size compares proportions within each group.
~B. Group A must have more total people#The bars are standardized, so total size may differ.
~C. Music caused Group A membership#The graph does not show causation.
~D. Group B has no music responses#A smaller segment may still be greater than zero.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
What is the first step when constructing a segmented bar graph from percentages?
{
~A. Draw bars with different total lengths#Segmented percent bars should have equal total length.
=B. Check that each group totals 100%#Each bar should represent the whole group.
~C. Ignore category labels#Labels are needed to identify segments.
~D. Convert every percent to a raw count#Raw counts are not needed if relative frequencies are already given.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A group has 8 car riders out of 20 students. What percent ride by car?
{
~A. 8%#The count is 8, not the percent.
~B. 20%#20 is the total count, not the percent.
=C. 40%#8 divided by 20 equals 0.40, or 40%.
~D. 60%#60% would be 12 out of 20.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Why can raw counts be misleading when groups have unequal totals?
{
=A. A larger count may come from a larger group, not a larger proportion#Relative frequency controls for group size.
~B. Counts can never be used in statistics#Counts are useful but may not be best for unequal groups.
~C. Percentages remove categories#Categories remain part of the display.
~D. Unequal groups cannot be compared at all#They can be compared with relative frequencies.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Which table can be used directly to construct a segmented bar graph?
{
~A. A list of unrelated numerical measurements#Segmented bars summarize categorical proportions.
~B. A table with no categories#Segments need categories.
~C. A scatterplot coordinate table#Scatterplots use paired numerical values.
=D. A table of percentages by group and category#Percentages give segment lengths directly.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
In Grade 9, bus is 50%; in Grade 10, bus is 45%. What is true?
{
~A. Grade 10 has a greater bus proportion#45% is less than 50%.
~B. Both bus proportions are equal#The values differ by 5 percentage points.
=C. Grade 9 has a slightly greater bus proportion#50% is greater than 45%.
~D. Bus is impossible to compare#The same category can be compared across groups.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
What does relative frequency mean?
{
=A. A part of a total, often written as a decimal or percent#Relative frequency compares a category to its group total.
~B. A count with no total#That is frequency, not relative frequency.
~C. A category name#Category names identify groups, not proportions.
~D. A cause-and-effect conclusion#Relative frequency is descriptive.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A segmented bar graph has unlabeled segments. What problem occurs?
{
=A. The viewer cannot tell which category each segment represents#Labels or a legend are needed to interpret segments.
~B. The percentages automatically become wrong#Missing labels do not change the values, but they make interpretation unclear.
~C. The graph becomes a scatterplot#Labels do not change the graph type.
~D. The bars no longer total 100%#Total length is separate from labeling.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A table shows 25%, 25%, 25%, and 25% for one group. How should the segments look?
{
~A. One segment should take the whole bar#Each category has only 25%.
=B. Four equal segments#Equal percentages produce equal segment lengths.
~C. The first segment should be longest#All percentages are equal.
~D. No graph can be drawn#A segmented bar can be drawn with equal segments.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
What comparison should be made from segmented bars?
{
~A. Exact individual scores#The display summarizes categories.
~B. Median and range#Those are numerical summary measures.
~C. Slope and intercept#Those belong to linear models.
=D. Category proportions across groups#Segmented bars compare relative frequencies.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A category has relative frequency 0.05. What percent of the bar is that?
{
~A. 50%#0.50 equals 50%, not 0.05.
~B. 0.5%#0.005 equals 0.5%.
=C. 5%#0.05 equals 5%.
~D. 95%#95% is the complement of 5%.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Which conclusion is supported if Group B has the largest car segment?
{
~A. Group B has the greatest total number of people#Segmented bars compare proportions, not necessarily totals.
~B. Car choice caused membership in Group B#The graph does not prove cause.
~C. No other group has car riders#Other groups may have smaller car segments.
=D. Group B has the greatest proportion choosing car#Largest segment means largest relative frequency for that category.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Which mistake would make a segmented bar graph misleading?
{
~A. Keeping category order consistent#That improves clarity.
~B. Labeling each segment#Labels support interpretation.
~C. Checking that each bar totals 100%#That is necessary.
=D. Using bars with different total lengths for percentage comparisons#Equal total bar lengths are needed when each bar represents 100%.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A relative frequency table has rows for two grades and columns for transportation categories. What does one row represent?
{
~A. A single numerical measurement#The row summarizes categorical proportions.
=B. The distribution of transportation categories within one grade#Each row shows category proportions for that group.
~C. A five-number summary#That belongs to numerical data.
~D. A cause-and-effect explanation#The row describes data; it does not explain causes.
}
